Subject: Expand .rept/.endr blocks
Origin: Filnix, https://github.com/mbrock/fil-c/commit/d6cfc13cc4ebc78240c19e34faa3aa4f4954284a
Upstream-Status: Pending; proposed for pizlonator/fil-c from branch mbrock:sarcasm-rept

`.rept` was treated like any other structural directive, which ends the
current data block. In

    TABLE:
        .rept 2
        .short 1, 2
        .endr

TABLE named an empty data object, and the repeated `.short`s fell into an
unlabeled block that the dead-content rule dropped without a diagnostic.
References to TABLE then failed to link.

sarcasm.compile now expands `.rept N` ... `.endr` (nesting allowed) before
any other analysis, so the repeated statements are seen exactly as GNU as
assembles them, in data and in code. Each copy is a fresh statement, since
later passes key per-statement state on statement identity. An unmatched
`.rept` or `.endr`, or a count that is not a non-negative integer constant,
is rejected.

